### Differentiating using the power rule

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}f'(x)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(x^{-2}\right)
\\\\
&=-2x^{-2-1}\qquad\gray{\text{The power rule}}
\\\\
&=-2x^{-3}
\end{align}$

### Evaluating $f'(x)$

So we found that $f'(x)=-2x^{-3}$, which can also be written as $-\dfrac{2}{x^3}$.

Now let's plug $\blueD{x=5}$:

$\begin{align}
-\dfrac{2}{(\blueD{5})^3}&=-\dfrac{2}{125}
\end{align}$
